All right.

23
00:01:21,170 --> 00:01:21,840
Let's jump right in.

24
00:01:22,030 --> 00:01:24,770
Phase one, laying the groundwork.

25
00:01:24,790 --> 00:01:27,230
This is about initiating and formalizing meetings.

26
00:01:27,290 --> 00:01:28,290
Starting blocks.

27
00:01:28,590 --> 00:01:33,760
And we're kicking off with what I think is the absolute foundation stone for organizing any meeting.

28
00:01:34,160 --> 00:01:36,260
The very start of getting people together.

29
00:01:36,380 --> 00:01:39,570
It's let's set a time to meet or, you know, let's set up a time to meet.

30
00:01:40,270 --> 00:01:41,990
This is really where it all begins, isn't it?

31
00:01:42,330 --> 00:01:44,150
Without this, nothing else happens.

32
00:01:44,130 --> 00:01:45,800
You've absolutely hit the nail on the head there.

33
00:01:46,160 --> 00:01:52,200
This phrase, basically, it just means organizing or choosing a specific time and date for a meeting.

34
00:01:52,360 --> 00:01:52,840
Simple enough.

35
00:01:53,200 --> 00:01:59,290
And it's good to know, actually, that both set a time and set up a time, well, they're completely interchangeable.

36
00:01:59,310 --> 00:02:01,340
Okay, so no need to stress over which one?

37
00:02:01,360 --> 00:02:02,460
Nope.

38
00:02:02,480 --> 00:02:06,120
Native speakers use both all the time, and they mean exactly the same thing.

39
00:02:06,160 --> 00:02:08,950
So, you know, don't get bogged down worrying about the right one.

40
00:02:08,990 --> 00:02:10,830
Just use whichever feels more natural to you.

41
00:02:11,450 --> 00:02:13,530
The key thing is the action it starts.

42
00:02:13,880 --> 00:02:17,750
Okay, so it sounds simple, almost, I don't know, basic.

43
00:02:18,290 --> 00:02:20,450
But what's the real purpose behind it?

44
00:02:20,470 --> 00:02:25,200
Why is it so vital, especially when you're trying to be effective in business English?

45
00:02:25,440 --> 00:02:33,970
That's a great question because a critical aspect people sometimes overlook is just how fundamental this first step is.

46
00:02:33,950 --> 00:02:41,940
It's the main way to make sure that you and the other person or people are not just aware of but are actively agreeing on the exact timing.

47
00:02:41,960 --> 00:02:43,620
It's an agreement, not just a suggestion.

48
00:02:43,720 --> 00:02:44,240
Precisely.

49
00:02:44,490 --> 00:02:45,310
It's not just polite.

50
00:02:45,390 --> 00:02:46,390
It's a proactive step.

51
00:02:46,410 --> 00:02:46,750
Yeah.

52
00:02:46,770 --> 00:02:48,530
And it fundamentally prevents scheduling clashes.

53
00:02:48,570 --> 00:02:50,230
It minimizes misunderstandings.

54
00:02:50,570 --> 00:02:55,380
And it just gets rid of that last minute scramble where people show up at the wrong time or not at all.

55
00:02:55,360 --> 00:02:56,240
Yeah, that's happened.

56
00:02:56,500 --> 00:03:01,310
Think of it like establishing a mutual understanding, a shared commitment right from the get-go.

57
00:03:01,790 --> 00:03:03,990
And this brings up an important question for you listening.

58
00:03:05,440 --> 00:03:08,320
How often do you take the initiative to schedule things?

59
00:03:09,320 --> 00:03:12,360
This phrase, let's set a time, it's your key.

60
00:03:13,040 --> 00:03:21,340
It's your key to confidently taking control of the calendar, showing leadership, and honestly, showing respect for everyone's time right from the start.

61
00:03:21,320 --> 00:03:24,580
That proactive element, yeah, that definitely signals professionalism.

62
00:03:24,700 --> 00:03:26,950
Instead of just waiting passively, you're driving it forward.

63
00:03:27,210 --> 00:03:27,630
Exactly.

64
00:03:27,670 --> 00:03:34,340
So, for instance, maybe after a quick chat in the hallway or an email chain, you could just smoothly say, okay, this sounds important.

65
00:03:34,680 --> 00:03:37,950
Let's set a time to meet and go over the project updates next week.

66
00:03:37,970 --> 00:03:38,530
Perfect.

67
00:03:38,510 --> 00:03:39,850
Or maybe after a sales call.

68
00:03:39,910 --> 00:03:40,580
Great discussion.

69
00:03:40,620 --> 00:03:41,620
I think we need a follow up.

70
00:03:41,660 --> 00:03:44,540
Let's set up a time to meet again next month to review progress.

71
00:03:45,100 --> 00:03:47,830
It feels very clear, very action oriented.

72
00:03:47,970 --> 00:03:49,750
And that clarity is just crucial.

73
00:03:50,160 --> 00:03:56,430
If you avoid this phrase or you're vague about timing, you can end up in that endless email back and forth, right?

74
00:03:56,610 --> 00:03:58,090
Just trying to find a slot.

75
00:03:58,070 --> 00:03:59,310
Oh, the scheduling ping pong.

76
00:03:59,390 --> 00:03:59,830
Yeah.

77
00:03:59,850 --> 00:04:03,080
Or worse, you assume a time's agreed when it actually hasn't been.

78
00:04:03,700 --> 00:04:11,930
Using let's set a time to meet clearly frames the meeting as like a deliberate valued thing, not just some afterthought.

79
00:04:11,950 --> 00:04:13,550
It sets a professional tone right away.

80
00:04:13,610 --> 00:04:13,830
Okay.

81
00:04:13,850 --> 00:04:14,570
It makes total sense.

82
00:04:15,010 --> 00:04:27,870
So moving on from choosing the time, once you've agreed maybe verbally or in a quick message on when to meet, our next phrase takes us to the, well, the logical and frankly essential next step.

83
00:04:27,850 --> 00:04:31,060
formalizing it, especially now with all the tech we use.

84
00:04:31,360 --> 00:04:33,690
Once you've set that time, how do you make it official?

85
00:04:33,750 --> 00:04:35,370
Make sure it's actually on everyone's radar.

86
00:04:35,730 --> 00:04:37,280
Lock it in as a concrete thing.

87
00:04:37,420 --> 00:04:37,640
Right.

88
00:04:37,940 --> 00:04:39,960
That's exactly where phrase number two comes in.

89
00:04:40,140 --> 00:04:41,410
I'll send out a calendar invite.

90
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:42,780
That's absolutely right.

91
00:04:42,960 --> 00:04:46,720
And in this context, you know, a calendar, it isn't just the paper thing on your wall.

92
00:04:46,800 --> 00:04:49,170
It's just another word for your digital schedule.

93
00:04:49,190 --> 00:04:49,850
Your outlook.

94
00:04:50,210 --> 00:04:50,370
Yeah.

95
00:04:50,390 --> 00:04:51,330
Google calendar.

96
00:04:51,530 --> 00:04:51,950
Exactly.

97
00:04:52,310 --> 00:05:04,790
That ever-present digital list of all your meetings, appointments, everything, and a calendar invite is that specific digital message, that notification that gets sent through those apps.

98
00:05:05,350 --> 00:05:07,970
Its purpose is super clear and highly functional.

99
00:05:08,130 --> 00:05:08,590
Oh, so.

100
00:05:09,420 --> 00:05:22,260
It ensures the meeting will automatically just pop onto your calendar and everyone else's at the exact time you agreed on, whether it's 9.00 a.m., 10.00 a.m., whenever it's there, a visible block of time you can see and plan around.

101
00:05:22,330 --> 00:05:25,670
Okay, that sounds like it really streamlines things, prevents a lot of headaches.

102
00:05:25,690 --> 00:05:26,370
Oh, absolutely.

103
00:05:26,570 --> 00:05:26,670
Yeah.

104
00:05:26,690 --> 00:05:30,330
And it's pretty much a non-negotiable step in professional English communication these days.

105
00:05:30,710 --> 00:05:35,380
This phrase signals that full integration of technology into our daily business routines.

106
00:05:35,640 --> 00:05:36,020
Right.

107
00:05:36,040 --> 00:05:42,930
It makes scheduling incredibly efficient, traceable, and, well, undeniably formal for everyone involved.

108
00:05:43,310 --> 00:05:46,030
It creates a clear, shareable, and actionable record.

109
00:05:46,190 --> 00:05:46,590
Actionable.

110
00:05:46,750 --> 00:05:47,690
It's not just a courtesy.

111
00:05:47,790 --> 00:05:52,330
It's really a critical piece of project management and team alignment.

112
00:05:52,310 --> 00:05:59,170
It helps prevent missed meetings because someone forgot a verbal agreement or lost a sticky note or just got overwhelmed.

113
00:05:59,990 --> 00:06:06,810
It's about precision and accountability in, let's face it, an increasingly digital and fast work world.

114
00:06:07,010 --> 00:06:10,180
That clarity and accountability, they're just invaluable.

115
00:06:10,420 --> 00:06:12,300
Think about the peace of mind this gives you.

116
00:06:12,340 --> 00:06:16,440
No more wondering if someone remembered the time right or if they even wrote it down.

117
00:06:16,550 --> 00:06:17,990
It's like a digital handshake, right?

118
00:06:18,430 --> 00:06:26,400
It solidifies your agreement and makes sure everyone has the same correct info, often including key things like the meeting link or the room number.

119
00:06:26,440 --> 00:06:27,000
Exactly.

120
00:06:27,020 --> 00:06:31,730
So after agreeing on a time, you'd confidently say something like, fantastic, that works for me.

121
00:06:31,790 --> 00:06:35,330
I'll send out a calendar invite for Thursday morning and I'll include the Zoom link.

122
00:06:35,310 --> 00:06:36,730
leaves no room for doubt.

123
00:06:36,850 --> 00:06:46,630
And, you know, beyond just the time and link, a really well-crafted calendar invite can also include the agenda, maybe links to relevant documents, or just a brief note on the meeting's purpose.

124
00:06:47,230 --> 00:06:48,790
Ah, so it preps people, too.

125
00:06:49,090 --> 00:06:49,430
Totally.

126
00:06:49,750 --> 00:06:54,660
It pre-populates everyone with the context they need, which leads to better preparation all around.

127
00:06:55,460 --> 00:07:01,810
It's really a foundational piece of organized collaboration, making sure you're all on the same page before you even start talking.

128
00:07:03,090 --> 00:07:04,170
Phase two.

129
00:07:04,610 --> 00:07:06,090
Clarifying and structuring.

130
00:07:06,590 --> 00:07:08,010
Understanding the meeting's core.

131
00:07:08,310 --> 00:07:08,590
All right.

132
00:07:08,670 --> 00:07:13,920
So we've efficiently handled the when and the how of getting a meeting formally on the books.

133
00:07:14,540 --> 00:07:16,380
Let's shift gears now into phase two.

134
00:07:17,040 --> 00:07:18,680
Clarifying and structuring.

135
00:07:18,700 --> 00:07:21,100
This is all about understanding the meeting's core.

136
00:07:21,300 --> 00:07:22,430
Getting into the what's.

137
00:07:22,450 --> 00:07:22,910
Exactly.

138
00:07:23,050 --> 00:07:25,870
Because knowing when you're meeting is only half the battle, right?

139
00:07:25,970 --> 00:07:27,870
You need to know what you're actually going to talk about.

140
00:07:28,330 --> 00:07:31,920
And that brings us straight to our third and equally crucial phrase.

141
00:07:32,880 --> 00:07:33,720
What's on the agenda?

142
00:07:33,800 --> 00:07:34,340
Indeed.

143
00:07:34,760 --> 00:07:40,410
The agenda, simply put, is the list of specific topics or items you're going to discuss during the meeting.

144
00:07:40,670 --> 00:07:48,780
So in essence, when you ask what's on the agenda, you're basically asking what's the main point of this meeting and what specific things will we cover?

145
00:07:49,400 --> 00:07:54,230
It's direct, it's professional, and it's a totally necessary way to get clear on the meeting's content.

146
00:07:54,890 --> 00:07:56,610
It's about seeking that structure.

147
00:07:57,230 --> 00:07:59,760
So it's not just like passive curiosity then.

148
00:07:59,970 --> 00:08:03,480
It sounds more like a proactive question aimed at really preparing yourself.

149
00:08:03,540 --> 00:08:04,220
Is that right?

150
00:08:04,240 --> 00:08:04,740
Precisely.

151
00:08:04,880 --> 00:08:12,000
This phrase is absolutely vital because it empowers you to be, well, intensely proactive in your preparation for English at work.

152
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:18,750
Seeing that list of topics before you even walk into the meeting, whether it's virtual or in person, is incredibly helpful.

153
00:08:18,890 --> 00:08:19,740
Why is that?

154
00:08:19,920 --> 00:08:24,050
It gives you that invaluable chance to spot any questions you might have beforehand.

155
00:08:24,730 --> 00:08:30,820
Or maybe you realize you need to do some quick research, gather some supporting data, or just organize materials you need to bring or share.

156
00:08:31,460 --> 00:08:32,000
Ah, okay.

157
00:08:32,480 --> 00:08:34,020
Avoids being put on the spot.

158
00:08:34,040 --> 00:08:34,600
Exactly.

159
00:08:35,210 --> 00:08:43,000
This proactive approach means you're not caught off guard, you can actually contribute meaningfully, and you sound thoroughly prepared.

160
00:08:43,780 --> 00:08:46,160
And this raises an important question for you listening.

161
00:08:46,970 --> 00:08:50,610
How does having an agenda actually empower you to contribute better?

162
00:08:51,230 --> 00:08:51,880
Good question.

163
00:08:52,170 --> 00:08:53,820
It's not just about knowing the topics.

164
00:08:53,920 --> 00:09:03,370
It's about strategically getting ready to engage, to speak confidently in your business English, and show that you've really thought about the meeting's goals.

165
00:09:03,690 --> 00:09:06,530
Yeah, I can imagine many of us have felt that discomfort, right?

166
00:09:06,710 --> 00:09:10,250
Walking into a meeting totally blind, wishing we knew what was coming.

167
00:09:11,090 --> 00:09:12,010
Happens all the time.

168
00:09:12,400 --> 00:09:18,290
This phrase, what's on the agenda, it's your direct line to feeling prepared, feeling in control.

169
00:09:18,790 --> 00:09:21,420
It turns potential anxiety into actual confidence.

170
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:27,150
For instance, if you get a meeting invite but it's vague, you could professionally reply, thanks for the invite.

171
00:09:27,450 --> 00:09:31,760
Just so I can come prepared, what's on the agenda for tomorrow's meeting shows initiative.

172
00:09:31,740 --> 00:09:32,300
Absolutely.

173
00:09:32,440 --> 00:09:33,920
And it doesn't just show initiative.

174
00:09:34,280 --> 00:09:39,390
It also signals to the person who organized the meeting that you value efficiency and preparation.

175
00:09:39,650 --> 00:09:40,270
Ah, interesting.

176
00:09:40,290 --> 00:09:46,640
It helps make sure the meeting itself stays focused and productive, avoiding those rambling discussions that go nowhere.

177
00:09:47,220 --> 00:09:50,400
The lack of an agenda often leads to unfocused meetings.

178
00:09:50,980 --> 00:09:58,270
So asking for one is a small but honestly powerful way to influence the meeting's effectiveness and your own contribution too.

179
00:09:58,250 --> 00:10:02,960
OK, building on that idea of having an agenda, just knowing the topics is a great start.

180
00:10:02,980 --> 00:10:04,540
But is that always enough?

181
00:10:04,560 --> 00:10:05,080
Often not.

182
00:10:05,260 --> 00:10:05,480
Right.

183
00:10:06,060 --> 00:10:12,010
What if you need to be absolutely certain to leave no stone unturned in your prep?

184
00:10:12,030 --> 00:10:16,240
Maybe you're about to send the agenda out yourself or perhaps it's before a really critical presentation.

185
00:10:17,040 --> 00:10:21,400
That leads us straight to phrase number four, a phrase that really embodies meticulousness.

186
00:10:21,980 --> 00:10:23,230
Let me double check the agenda.

187
00:10:23,310 --> 00:10:23,510
Yes.

188
00:10:24,150 --> 00:10:26,990
This phrase is all about thoroughness and readiness.

189
00:10:26,970 --> 00:10:30,240
which is a hallmark of really excellent professional practice in English.

190
00:10:30,760 --> 00:10:40,170
To double check, it means looking at the agenda again or checking it one more time, but with a specific kind of intense focus, scrutinizing every single point closely.

191
00:10:40,650 --> 00:10:45,800
You want to make sure you understand every item and that you're truly unequivocally ready for everything that might come up.

192
00:10:46,210 --> 00:10:49,990
It's proactive error prevention and confidence building at its best.

193
00:10:50,040 --> 00:10:52,610
It sounds like it's about much more than just catching a typo.

194
00:10:52,640 --> 00:10:54,270
It's about being ready under pressure.

195
00:10:54,290 --> 00:10:55,070
It is.

196
00:10:55,750 --> 00:10:56,860
Think about the stakes, right?

197
00:10:57,340 --> 00:11:06,480
If your boss or a key client or maybe some critical stakeholder asks a tough question about a specific point on that agenda, you need to be ready.

198
00:11:06,460 --> 00:11:10,160
Ready to respond confidently, accurately, without hesitation.

199
00:11:10,450 --> 00:11:12,630
Double-checking ensures that level of preparedness.

200
00:11:12,970 --> 00:11:20,180
It also gives you that crucial chance to spot any typos, yes, but also factual errors or maybe make necessary changes before the meeting starts.

201
00:11:20,260 --> 00:11:20,400
Right.

202
00:11:20,480 --> 00:11:21,440
Fix it beforehand.

203
00:11:21,480 --> 00:11:25,530
This diligence helps you avoid that awkward, unprofessional...

204
00:11:25,510 --> 00:11:27,030
I don't know, moment.

205
00:11:27,630 --> 00:11:32,600
And it showcases your unwavering attention to detail, your readiness for work in English.

206
00:11:33,340 --> 00:11:35,040
And we connect this to the bigger picture, right?

207
00:11:35,060 --> 00:11:36,520
This isn't just about dodging mistakes.

208
00:11:36,900 --> 00:11:44,650
It's about building trust, enhancing your reputation for reliability and demonstrating meticulousness in your professional role.

209
00:11:45,130 --> 00:11:47,530
These are crucial things when you're doing business in English.

210
00:11:47,650 --> 00:11:56,420
Think of this as your final quality control step, your last line of defense before a big presentation or a really important high stakes discussion.

211
00:11:56,680 --> 00:11:56,850
Exactly.

212
00:11:56,970 --> 00:12:03,900
It's a habit that just speaks volumes about your attention to detail, your commitment to excellence, your overall professionalism.

213
00:12:04,240 --> 00:12:12,830
For example, if you're the organizer, you might say, okay, before I send this out to the team, let me just double check the agenda quickly and make sure all points are covered and accurate.

214
00:12:12,870 --> 00:12:13,690
Good practice.

215
00:12:13,670 --> 00:12:19,320
Or if you're attending, right, I've reviewed it, but let me double check the agenda against my notes just to be absolutely sure.

216
00:12:19,460 --> 00:12:20,140
Smart move.

217
00:12:20,160 --> 00:12:20,800
Okay.

218
00:12:20,820 --> 00:12:28,310
So once you've understood the agenda, you've thoroughly reviewed it, made sure everything's accurate and clear, what's the very next step?

219
00:12:28,960 --> 00:12:36,270
How do you make sure your message, your own contribution, lands clearly, concisely, and with maximum impact?

220
00:12:36,850 --> 00:12:38,530
This brings us to phrase number five.

221
00:12:39,170 --> 00:12:40,710
We need to prepare some talking points.

222
00:12:41,030 --> 00:12:41,970
Talking points.

223
00:12:42,540 --> 00:12:50,420
Yeah, these are essentially the key ideas, the main messages, the core arguments that you absolutely want to discuss, convey or emphasize during the meeting.

224
00:12:50,560 --> 00:12:51,950
The must-haves.

225
00:12:52,100 --> 00:12:52,760
Exactly.

226
00:12:53,220 --> 00:12:59,770
Sometimes these critical talking points might even be listed right there on the agenda itself, maybe as sub items.

227
00:13:00,610 --> 00:13:04,140
But their main job is to act as strategic anchors for your discussion.

228
00:13:04,380 --> 00:13:04,720
Anchors.

229
00:13:04,780 --> 00:13:05,240
I like that.

230
00:13:05,400 --> 00:13:05,700
Yeah.

231
00:13:05,720 --> 00:13:12,650
They ensure that critical information gets across, that your message stays focused and that you hit all the necessary points you plan to make.

232
00:13:13,050 --> 00:13:19,080
So they're not just a laundry list of everything you could say, but the distilled strategic points you must make.

233
00:13:19,240 --> 00:13:19,720
Precisely.

234
00:13:20,540 --> 00:13:22,710
They have significant strategic value.

235
00:13:23,430 --> 00:13:36,200
They act as a guide, whether it's mental or written down, helping you stay on track, articulate your thoughts clearly, especially under pressure, and make sure the most important information is communicated clearly and, hopefully, memorably.

236
00:13:37,540 --> 00:13:44,210
For non-native English speakers, prepping these points is really a fundamental part of mastering meeting prep in business English.

237
00:13:44,190 --> 00:13:52,230
It lets you deliver your message with impact and helps you avoid getting lost in translation or just rambling on with extra details.

238
00:13:52,250 --> 00:13:59,670
What's really fascinating here is how talking points kind of elegantly bridge that gap between preparation and actual delivery.

239
00:13:59,650 --> 00:14:01,130
Preparation and delivery.

240
00:14:01,150 --> 00:14:05,150
Yeah, ensuring clarity, focus, and conciseness in how you communicate.

241
00:14:05,200 --> 00:14:10,360
It's about being impactful and efficient, which are qualities highly valued everywhere, right?

242
00:14:10,520 --> 00:14:10,960
Absolutely.

243
00:14:11,440 --> 00:14:15,550
Imagine walking into a meeting knowing exactly the core messages you want to hit.

244
00:14:16,050 --> 00:14:20,270
Feeling confident you won't forget anything crucial, even if the conversation takes a turn.

245
00:14:20,580 --> 00:14:21,540
That's the power.

246
00:14:21,520 --> 00:14:24,320
That's the empowering force of preparing talking points for you.

247
00:14:25,120 --> 00:14:33,430
For example, if you're working on a presentation with someone, you might suggest, okay, before we finalize the deck, let's prepare some clear talking points for the sales update meeting.

248
00:14:33,980 --> 00:14:35,920
Let's focus on the Q3 growth numbers.

249
00:14:36,220 --> 00:14:36,760
Good idea.

250
00:14:36,980 --> 00:14:40,810
Or if you're prepping for a one-on-one, right, I'm meeting the CEO next week.

251
00:14:41,150 --> 00:14:45,850
I really need to prepare my talking points to make sure I convey our team's wins and what we need going forward.

252
00:14:45,830 --> 00:14:49,640
And this prep, it isn't just for super formal presentations either.

253
00:14:50,160 --> 00:14:59,490
Even for like a casual check-in meeting, having just a few mental talking points helps you use the time effectively and leave a really positive lasting impression.

254
00:15:00,090 --> 00:15:02,560
It's about being intentional with your words.

255
00:15:02,580 --> 00:15:08,580
Three, phase three, data presentation and inclusivity refining and expanding.

256
00:15:08,680 --> 00:15:12,650
Okay, let's move into phase three, data, presentation, and inclusivity.

257
00:15:13,170 --> 00:15:16,640
This is about refining and expanding your meeting prep strategies.

258
00:15:16,680 --> 00:15:17,560
Taking it up a level.

259
00:15:17,710 --> 00:15:17,950
Yeah.

260
00:15:18,350 --> 00:15:22,300
Often, business meetings aren't just about abstract ideas or a general discussion.

261
00:15:22,360 --> 00:15:25,580
They're fundamentally driven by concrete data, by evidence.

262
00:15:25,600 --> 00:15:26,290
Numbers matter.

263
00:15:26,270 --> 00:15:26,910
Exactly.

264
00:15:27,090 --> 00:15:31,580
This brings us to phrase number six, a phrase that signals diligent research and preparation.

265
00:15:31,640 --> 00:15:33,340
I've pulled the latest numbers.

266
00:15:33,480 --> 00:15:34,550
This is an incredibly common phrase.

267
00:15:34,950 --> 00:15:35,590
Very efficient.

268
00:15:35,950 --> 00:15:37,490
Very professional business English.

269
00:15:37,930 --> 00:15:40,040
Pulled, here, it's used idiomatically.

270
00:15:40,140 --> 00:15:43,270
It just means gathered, collected, or retrieved.

271
00:15:43,290 --> 00:15:43,410
Okay.

272
00:15:43,430 --> 00:15:44,730
Pulled equals gathered.

273
00:15:44,950 --> 00:15:45,710
Yeah.

274
00:15:45,730 --> 00:15:49,870
And when we talk about numbers in a business context, we usually mean quantitative data.

275
00:15:50,490 --> 00:15:59,340
Things like sales figures, quarterly profit margins, market share stats, project completion rates, maybe customer engagement metrics.

276
00:15:59,600 --> 00:16:00,520
The hard data.

277
00:16:00,540 --> 00:16:00,990
Exactly.

278
00:16:01,330 --> 00:16:02,430
And it's not just any data.

279
00:16:02,610 --> 00:16:08,470
It's the specific, most current and most relevant factual support for whatever you're discussing.

280
00:16:08,490 --> 00:16:15,440
So it's about having all that critical info, not just gathered, but actually ready, ready to present and use as proof.

281
00:16:15,460 --> 00:16:15,900
Precisely.

282
00:16:16,320 --> 00:16:20,570
This phrase clearly conveys that you've methodically gone through the relevant data sources.

283
00:16:21,110 --> 00:16:24,880
You've organized that info, maybe analyzed it and compiled it all together.

284
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:26,040
How might that look?

285
00:16:26,120 --> 00:16:31,810
perhaps into a detailed spreadsheet, maybe a compelling graph, or even integrated right into a presentation deck.

286
00:16:32,360 --> 00:16:40,250
It's basically a verbal signal that the information is now available and prepped, ready to be shared with clients, shown to your boss, or discussed with colleagues.

287
00:16:40,350 --> 00:16:42,480
So it signals readiness with facts.

288
00:16:42,590 --> 00:16:42,890
Yes.

289
00:16:43,670 --> 00:16:51,930
Readiness backed by concrete evidence-based information, which is really a cornerstone of effective business communication in English.

290
00:16:52,250 --> 00:17:01,700
And connecting this to the bigger picture again, I've pulled the latest numbers, signifies that crucial shift from just talking conceptually to making evidence-based decisions.

291
00:17:01,940 --> 00:17:02,300
Right.

292
00:17:02,280 --> 00:17:12,990
It actively highlights how important data literacy is in today's business world, and maybe more importantly, your ability to find, understand, and talk about that data.

293
00:17:13,050 --> 00:17:13,670
That makes sense.

294
00:17:13,950 --> 00:17:17,480
For you, the listener, this phrase isn't just about presenting raw figures.

295
00:17:17,960 --> 00:17:20,960
It's intrinsically tied to building your credibility.

296
00:17:21,730 --> 00:17:22,210
Absolutely.

297
00:17:22,230 --> 00:17:27,060
It tells everyone in the room you've done your homework, you're thorough, and you're ready to back up your points with actual facts.

298
00:17:27,180 --> 00:17:32,020
It moves the conversation from, well, I think, to, okay, the data shows.

299
00:17:32,830 --> 00:17:34,150
That's a powerful shift.

300
00:17:34,130 --> 00:17:34,830
It really is.

301
00:17:35,090 --> 00:17:41,280
You might confidently state, OK, before the strategic review, I've pulled the latest numbers from last quarter's report and they indicate a pretty clear trend.

302
00:17:42,080 --> 00:17:47,180
Or for our budget discussion later, I've pulled the latest numbers on our spending and we need to address a few areas.

303
00:17:47,600 --> 00:17:49,510
The strategic impact there is huge.

304
00:17:50,150 --> 00:17:57,010
It positions you as someone who doesn't just rely on gut feelings or assumptions, but on verifiable information.

305
00:17:56,990 --> 00:18:02,760
That fosters trust and enables much more informed discussions and ultimately better decisions.

306
00:18:03,280 --> 00:18:08,510
It's really an indispensable phrase for anyone presenting analysis or making data-driven proposals.

307
00:18:09,110 --> 00:18:18,020
Okay, building on presenting data, our discussions often need a strong visual element, right, to convey complex information effectively.

308
00:18:18,260 --> 00:18:19,220
Visuals help a lot.

309
00:18:19,490 --> 00:18:25,240
So you've diligently gathered your data, you've refined your talking points, and now you've put them into a presentation.

310
00:18:25,820 --> 00:18:34,900
How do you make absolutely sure that your presentation looks as polished and professional as your content, avoiding those last-minute glitches or, you know, embarrassing errors?

311
00:18:34,920 --> 00:18:36,000
A visual check, yes.

312
00:18:36,020 --> 00:18:37,580
This brings us to phrase number seven.

313
00:18:37,820 --> 00:18:39,510
Let me run through the slides one more time.

314
00:18:39,540 --> 00:18:44,820
To run through something in this specific context means to quickly but thoroughly review it.

315
00:18:45,300 --> 00:18:47,810
It's very similar to double-checking, which we talk about.

316
00:18:48,170 --> 00:18:52,900
But it's typically applied to the sequential review of a presentation, especially one with visual aids.

317
00:18:53,440 --> 00:19:00,410
And slides, of course, are just the individual images, charts, bullet points, pictures, whatever, that make up your digital presentation deck.

318
00:19:00,550 --> 00:19:01,050
And the goal.

319
00:19:01,290 --> 00:19:03,550
The goal is simple but critical.

320
00:19:04,070 --> 00:19:14,390
You absolutely do not want any spelling mistakes, grammatical errors, weird formatting, or just plain wrong information on those slides that could distract people from your message.

321
00:19:14,410 --> 00:19:15,260
Oh, the horror.

322
00:19:15,280 --> 00:19:20,430
A glaring typo on the title slide or a chart that's completely misaligned.

323
00:19:20,450 --> 00:19:21,490
Cringeworthy.

324
00:19:21,470 --> 00:19:22,590
We've all seen it, right?

325
00:19:23,010 --> 00:19:30,480
That momentary cringe that pulls everyone's attention away from your brilliant content and focuses it right onto a preventable mistake.

326
00:19:30,500 --> 00:19:31,000
Exactly.

327
00:19:31,320 --> 00:19:34,770
So running through the slides one more time isn't just about catching errors.

328
00:19:34,870 --> 00:19:42,320
It's really your last line of defense against those small, avoidable distractions that can totally undermine an otherwise great presentation.

329
00:19:43,280 --> 00:19:46,480
It's about ensuring your delivery is as polished as your ideas.

330
00:19:46,960 --> 00:19:47,780
Precisely.

331
00:19:48,250 --> 00:19:54,990
This phrase indicates that crucial final quality check, like a meticulous pre-flight checklist for your presentation.

332
00:19:55,630 --> 00:20:04,690
It's about making sure everything is perfect before the meeting starts so your visual aids actually enhance rather than detract from your carefully crafted message.

333
00:20:04,670 --> 00:20:06,750
And that attention to detail reflects well.

334
00:20:06,950 --> 00:20:07,450
Absolutely.

335
00:20:07,810 --> 00:20:14,140
What's really fascinating here is that implicit understanding of how important presentation polish is in professional settings.

336
00:20:14,600 --> 00:20:19,010
It's not just about what you say, but how you present it visually, verbally, even non-verbally.

337
00:20:19,570 --> 00:20:28,740
This kind of unwavering attention to detail reflects incredibly well on you professionally, especially when communicating in English where clarity and precision are so highly valued.

338
00:20:28,900 --> 00:20:36,820
For you, the listener, this means actively preventing those embarrassing typos, misspellings, or misalignments that can instantly derail your audience's focus.

339
00:20:37,330 --> 00:20:41,380
It's about projecting unwavering professionalism right down to the smallest details.

340
00:20:41,460 --> 00:20:49,990
You might say, okay, before the big client pitch tomorrow, I just need to grab a coffee and then let me run through the slides one more time just to catch any lingering issues.

341
00:20:50,730 --> 00:20:52,700
It really shows your commitment to excellence.

342
00:20:52,720 --> 00:20:53,020
It does.

343
00:20:53,280 --> 00:20:53,520
Okay.

344
00:20:54,100 --> 00:20:59,630
Moving beyond just your own individual prep, meetings aren't always a solo performance.

345
00:20:59,670 --> 00:21:01,160
In fact, they rarely are, right?

346
00:21:01,180 --> 00:21:02,320
It's actually collaborative.

347
00:21:02,430 --> 00:21:10,370
Sometimes to get the best result, to get a truly well-rounded perspective, you genuinely need more voices, more viewpoints.

348
00:21:11,070 --> 00:21:14,330
Or maybe just more information needs to be shared with key people.

349
00:21:15,190 --> 00:21:15,310
Right.

350
00:21:15,330 --> 00:21:20,060
This broadens our scope now, thinking about wider team involvement, strategic inclusion.

351
00:21:20,100 --> 00:21:22,380
And that leads us to phrase number eight.

352
00:21:23,290 --> 00:21:25,050
Do we need to loop anyone else in?

353
00:21:25,520 --> 00:21:26,960
This is a very modern phrase.

354
00:21:27,300 --> 00:21:29,520
And frankly, it's everywhere in contemporary business English.

355
00:21:29,920 --> 00:21:36,990
To loop someone in means to inform them about a specific discussion or meeting, to actively include them in the conversation.

356
00:21:37,330 --> 00:21:42,600
And often it means inviting them to be part of the discussion or contribute their expertise.

357
00:21:43,460 --> 00:21:50,010
It inherently implies you think their input is valuable or that they absolutely need to know what's going on for their own work.

358
00:21:50,290 --> 00:21:57,740
So it's about making sure the right people are in the room or at least in the know for the best collaboration and decision making.

359
00:21:58,120 --> 00:21:58,540
Exactly.

360
00:21:59,220 --> 00:22:12,670
Before a meeting or even during an ongoing project discussion, you often have to strategically decide who should attend or be informed because their contribution, their insights, maybe their specific expertise would be invaluable.

361
00:22:12,890 --> 00:22:13,140
Right.

362
00:22:13,380 --> 00:22:19,720
This phrase helps make that critical decision process happen by asking, do we need to loop anyone else in?

363
00:22:19,960 --> 00:22:21,200
You're not just inviting people.

364
00:22:21,540 --> 00:22:26,180
You're actually engaging in strategic team building and managing the flow of information.

365
00:22:26,640 --> 00:22:27,100
Interesting.

366
00:22:27,120 --> 00:22:28,120
And you mentioned the opposite.

367
00:22:28,280 --> 00:22:33,470
Yeah, the opposite is being out of the loop, which means you're not included or informed about something important.

368
00:22:33,870 --> 00:22:39,230
So if you find yourself in that spot, you might politely say something like, hey, sorry, I think I'm out of the loop on that project.

369
00:22:39,270 --> 00:22:40,280
Could you loop me in, please?

370
00:22:40,560 --> 00:22:41,640
Ah, okay.

371
00:22:41,660 --> 00:22:42,520
Good to know both sides.

372
00:22:43,040 --> 00:22:46,840
And this raises an important question for you about collaboration and team dynamics, right?

373
00:22:47,900 --> 00:22:49,960
In a world drowning in information,

374
00:22:50,160 --> 00:22:50,600
Yeah.

375
00:22:51,420 --> 00:22:58,640
Judiciously looping people in, make sure everyone has the necessary context without being totally overwhelmed.

376
00:22:59,200 --> 00:23:05,170
It fosters incredibly efficient teamwork and really effective business English communication.

377
00:23:05,620 --> 00:23:12,320
Have you ever felt out of the loop, wishing you'd been included in a key discussion or decision that directly affected your work?

378
00:23:12,340 --> 00:23:13,300
We probably all have.

379
00:23:14,320 --> 00:23:21,540
This phrase empowers you to ensure everyone who needs to be informed is informed, preventing future misunderstandings or roadblocks.

380
00:23:21,700 --> 00:23:23,020
You can use it proactively.

381
00:23:23,170 --> 00:23:29,140
Suggest something like, before we finalize the project plan, do we need to loop anyone else in from marketing for their perspective?

382
00:23:29,540 --> 00:23:33,640
Or if you're the one needing the info, hey, I overheard you discussing the new client proposal.

383
00:23:33,720 --> 00:23:35,960
Could you loop me in on the details so I'm up to speed?

384
00:23:36,380 --> 00:23:38,630
It just promotes transparency and teamwork.

385
00:23:38,930 --> 00:23:40,850
And its power isn't just about inclusion.

386
00:23:41,010 --> 00:23:42,530
Sometimes it's about exclusion, too.

387
00:23:43,650 --> 00:23:48,580
Asking this question can also help you clarify who doesn't need to be in the meeting.

388
00:23:48,720 --> 00:23:49,940
Saving people's time.

389
00:23:50,140 --> 00:23:50,680
Exactly.

390
00:23:51,280 --> 00:23:55,950
Saving valuable time for those whose input isn't directly needed for this specific discussion.

391
00:23:55,970 --> 00:23:59,380
It's really about optimizing attendance for maximum productivity.

392
00:23:59,400 --> 00:24:00,360
4.

393
00:24:01,000 --> 00:24:01,480
Phase 4.

394
00:24:02,110 --> 00:24:05,610
Final touches and alignment, ensuring seamless execution.

395
00:24:05,590 --> 00:24:07,080
Okay, we're heading into the home stretch now.

396
00:24:07,180 --> 00:24:08,980
Phase four, final touches and alignment.

397
00:24:09,540 --> 00:24:12,670
This is about ensuring seamless execution.

398
00:24:12,830 --> 00:24:13,730
Bringing it all together.

399
00:24:13,870 --> 00:24:20,860
As we get closer to the actual meeting time, sometimes you just need information fast, right to the point, without getting bogged down in all the details.

400
00:24:20,940 --> 00:24:22,510
Time is often really tight.

401
00:24:22,610 --> 00:24:23,590
Yeah, efficiency matters.

402
00:24:23,830 --> 00:24:25,670
This brings us to phrase number nine.

403
00:24:26,090 --> 00:24:27,540
Can you prep a quick summary?

404
00:24:27,630 --> 00:24:28,010
Prep.

405
00:24:28,170 --> 00:24:33,220
Yeah, this is a very common, very efficient, and slightly informal short version of prepare.

406
00:24:33,440 --> 00:24:35,750
Okay, prep means prepare but faster.

407
00:24:35,950 --> 00:24:36,270
Kind of.

408
00:24:36,330 --> 00:24:43,260
The nuance with prep versus the full prepare is that prep often implies speed, simplicity, immediacy.

409
00:24:44,000 --> 00:24:45,500
It sort of sounds like we're going fast.

410
00:24:45,520 --> 00:24:48,290
Like just something simple, something easy to digest quickly.

411
00:24:48,410 --> 00:24:48,750
Got it.

412
00:24:49,090 --> 00:24:49,810
And the summary.

413
00:24:49,960 --> 00:24:56,490
A summary is just the main idea, the key points, the essence of a meeting or a document or a discussion.

414
00:24:56,930 --> 00:24:59,680
Not every single little word that was said, not every tiny detail.

415
00:24:59,880 --> 00:25:04,530
So it's about distilling the absolute core message, not recreating the whole thing.

416
00:25:04,590 --> 00:25:07,560
It's really about respecting everyone's time and attention span.

417
00:25:07,780 --> 00:25:08,880
Exactly.

418
00:25:08,860 --> 00:25:26,310
The explicit goal here is to get something that someone, maybe your manager, maybe a busy stakeholder, maybe a team member who missed an earlier chat, can read or hear very quickly and immediately grasp what the meeting or the project update or whatever is about without needing to dive into every single granular detail.

419
00:25:26,430 --> 00:25:27,450
Rapid comprehension.

420
00:25:27,590 --> 00:25:28,670
Precisely.

421
00:25:28,650 --> 00:25:31,940
It's for rapid comprehension and efficiency in your communication.

422
00:25:32,200 --> 00:25:36,180
It's fairly vital in a busy work environment where you might be communicating in English.

423
00:25:36,820 --> 00:25:41,470
And what's fascinating here is that implicit understanding of speed and efficiency in modern business talk.

424
00:25:42,070 --> 00:25:48,420
It's about delivering maximum value quickly when you know your audience has limited time and just needs the key info now.

425
00:25:49,080 --> 00:25:56,330
For you, the listener, this is about getting the gist without getting lost in unnecessary details, providing that essential high-level overview.

426
00:25:56,480 --> 00:26:04,190
It respects everyone's precious time and ensures key information is accessible for quick review, maybe quick decision making.

427
00:26:04,650 --> 00:26:11,990
For example, if your manager asks about a complex client meeting you just finished, you might hear them say, OK, just give me the highlights.

428
00:26:12,080 --> 00:26:17,360
Can you prep a quick summary of the client's key concerns for the intro to our follow up call?

429
00:26:17,600 --> 00:26:18,320
Perfect example.

430
00:26:18,560 --> 00:26:25,510
Or right before the weekly team stand up tomorrow, can you prep a quick summary of the project's progress this week?

431
00:26:26,110 --> 00:26:28,930
It's also a really excellent way to check your own understanding.

432
00:26:29,680 --> 00:26:36,120
If you can summarize something complex, quickly and accurately, it really proves you've grasped the core concepts yourself.

433
00:26:36,200 --> 00:26:38,250
It's a vital skill in professional English.

434
00:26:38,390 --> 00:26:38,910
Absolutely.

435
00:26:39,350 --> 00:26:48,080
Okay, and now, for that absolute certainty, that feeling of being 100% prepared, totally aligned, ready to face whatever the meeting throws at you.

436
00:26:48,200 --> 00:26:49,200
The final check.

437
00:26:49,180 --> 00:26:55,200
There's one last incredibly important phrase, one that acts like the ultimate safety net, our tenth and final phrase.

438
00:26:55,900 --> 00:26:57,670
Let's touch base before the meeting.

439
00:26:58,070 --> 00:26:59,480
Let's touch base.

440
00:26:59,580 --> 00:26:59,980
Yes.

441
00:27:00,660 --> 00:27:03,740
A widely used, almost indispensable business English idiom.

442
00:27:03,800 --> 00:27:06,100
It simply means let's contact each other.

443
00:27:06,440 --> 00:27:07,310
Contact how?

444
00:27:07,470 --> 00:27:09,190
Well, the contact could take many forms.

445
00:27:09,730 --> 00:27:15,180
It could be talking face to face for a minute, having a quick chat online, sending a brief message, maybe a short phone call.

446
00:27:16,040 --> 00:27:19,720
The specific way isn't as important as the deliberate act of just connecting.

447
00:27:20,290 --> 00:27:23,710
It's about creating that moment for final synchronizations.

448
00:27:23,690 --> 00:27:24,350
Synchronization.

449
00:27:24,410 --> 00:27:27,860
OK, so what's the precise objective of this final connection?

450
00:27:28,000 --> 00:27:30,440
Is it just a formality or is there a deeper purpose?

451
00:27:30,720 --> 00:27:32,370
Oh, there's a profound objective.

452
00:27:33,030 --> 00:27:43,770
The goal of touching base is to just confirm or swiftly check any last minute details, clarify any lingering questions or address any unforeseen issues right before the main meeting kicks off.

453
00:27:43,950 --> 00:27:45,210
Like what specifically?

454
00:27:45,190 --> 00:27:50,890
It could involve verifying you both have the right slide deck, you know, the latest version, not some old one.

455
00:27:51,500 --> 00:27:55,460
It might mean confirming if someone double-checked the agenda for any final mistakes or updates.

456
00:27:55,960 --> 00:27:58,460
It's about asking, are you ready for the meeting?

457
00:27:58,660 --> 00:27:59,790
Do you have everything you need?

458
00:28:00,090 --> 00:28:06,290
The ultimate goal of touching base is to make sure we are aligned, which is another crucial business phrase.

459
00:28:06,310 --> 00:28:14,280
It means confirming that you are all on the same page, working towards the same goals, and that hopefully nothing unexpected will pop up during the meeting itself.

460
00:28:14,260 --> 00:28:15,950
Avoiding surprises.

461
00:28:16,000 --> 00:28:16,420
Exactly.

