OK, so you've asked maybe, hey, what's on the agenda?

706
00:41:01,030 --> 00:41:01,690
And you've received it.

707
00:41:01,950 --> 00:41:02,370
Now what?

708
00:41:03,010 --> 00:41:04,360
Is a quick scan enough?

709
00:41:05,000 --> 00:41:06,980
Probably not, especially if the stakes are high.

710
00:41:07,400 --> 00:41:11,330
Rarely is a quick scan truly sufficient, especially for complex topics.

711
00:41:11,410 --> 00:41:11,730
Right.

712
00:41:11,850 --> 00:41:13,430
And that's where our fourth phrase comes in.

713
00:41:13,490 --> 00:41:15,210
Perfectly designed for that deeper look.

714
00:41:15,270 --> 00:41:16,950
Let me double check the agenda.

715
00:41:16,970 --> 00:41:17,980
Ignal's thoroughness.

716
00:41:18,160 --> 00:41:29,320
Our research really emphasizes that double check means looking again, meticulously, reviewing for full understanding, spotting potential errors, maybe noticing last minute tweaks or additions.

717
00:41:29,340 --> 00:41:30,780
It's about attention to detail.

718
00:41:31,140 --> 00:41:40,640
And the guide explicitly links this to avoiding that awful, ah, I don't know, moment when your boss or a client asks about item 3B on the agenda.

719
00:41:40,720 --> 00:41:42,400
we've all felt that cold sweat potential.

720
00:41:42,660 --> 00:41:43,080
Exactly.

721
00:41:43,120 --> 00:41:48,650
This phrase is about moving from just being prepared to being flawlessly ready.

722
00:41:49,230 --> 00:41:52,780
An example might be, OK, got the draft agenda.

723
00:41:52,840 --> 00:41:58,700
Let me just double check it before I confirm, or let me double check the agenda points against my notes.

724
00:41:58,720 --> 00:42:02,430
This phrase really hammers home the importance of thoroughness and accuracy.

725
00:42:03,250 --> 00:42:07,120
In professional life, those things matter immensely.

726
00:42:07,100 --> 00:42:10,340
Double checking implies a detailed critical review.

727
00:42:10,620 --> 00:42:11,640
It's not a quick glance.

728
00:42:11,760 --> 00:42:13,620
It's an active process of verification.

729
00:42:13,780 --> 00:42:14,640
To avoid mistakes.

730
00:42:15,080 --> 00:42:16,340
To avoid miscommunication.

731
00:42:16,640 --> 00:42:21,250
To avoid the embarrassment of presenting wrong information or overlooking a key detail.

732
00:42:21,570 --> 00:42:23,650
But it's also proactive problem solving.

733
00:42:23,710 --> 00:42:24,330
How so?

734
00:42:24,470 --> 00:42:28,010
By taking the initiative to double check it, you might catch an error someone else made.

735
00:42:28,700 --> 00:42:30,840
You might identify a crucial point that's missing.

736
00:42:31,180 --> 00:42:35,700
You might even see a way to improve the flow or suggest adding something before the meeting starts.

737
00:42:35,750 --> 00:42:38,830
Ah, so you're contributing to the meeting's quality even before it begins.

738
00:42:39,010 --> 00:42:39,530
Absolutely.

739
00:42:39,750 --> 00:42:45,420
You're saving time, preventing potential confusion later, and showing you have a really keen eye for detail.

740
00:42:45,680 --> 00:42:46,460
That gets noticed.

741
00:42:46,880 --> 00:42:49,300
And personally, it helps you prepare better too, right?

742
00:42:49,400 --> 00:42:49,800
Definitely.

743
00:42:49,840 --> 00:42:52,050
It lets you anticipate those specific questions.

744
00:42:52,690 --> 00:43:00,070
If you see Q3 financials on the agenda, double-checking might prompt you to think, okay, what specific questions will the CFO ask about this?

745
00:43:00,700 --> 00:43:03,060
Do I have the breakdown by region ready?

746
00:43:03,040 --> 00:43:05,980
So you have the answers ready, or at least you're prepared to find them quickly.

747
00:43:06,040 --> 00:43:08,610
It's your final defense against being caught unprepared.

748
00:43:08,840 --> 00:43:09,430
Exactly.

749
00:43:09,950 --> 00:43:11,670
It's not just about avoiding your own errors.

750
00:43:12,110 --> 00:43:14,430
It's collaborative quality control, as you put it earlier.

751
00:43:14,890 --> 00:43:23,330
It empowers you to ensure your preparation is top-notch, and it positions you as meticulous, reliable, detail-oriented.

752
00:43:23,710 --> 00:43:27,890
It's a key tactic to avoid those awkward moments and project confidence.

753
00:43:28,250 --> 00:43:29,640
It shows you care about getting it right.

754
00:43:30,050 --> 00:43:30,690
Fantastic.

755
00:43:30,810 --> 00:43:31,880
Okay, path so far.

756
00:43:32,260 --> 00:43:35,520
Time set, invite sent, agenda understood, agenda double-checked.

757
00:43:35,700 --> 00:43:40,610
We're moving now from just understand the roadmap to actively crafting your specific input.

758
00:43:40,810 --> 00:43:42,590
Getting ready to contribute substantively.

759
00:43:42,690 --> 00:43:43,190
Precisely.

760
00:43:43,530 --> 00:43:45,110
Which brings us to phrase number five.

761
00:43:45,180 --> 00:43:47,500
We need to prepare some talking points.

762
00:43:47,700 --> 00:43:49,200
Ah, the talking points.

763
00:43:49,220 --> 00:43:49,620
Essential.

764
00:43:49,900 --> 00:43:54,510
Our guide defines these as the key ideas you want to discuss.

765
00:43:54,490 --> 00:44:01,160
They might even be listed on the agenda or they might be the specific things you want to make sure you cover under a broader agenda item.

766
00:44:01,640 --> 00:44:02,460
Your core messages.

767
00:44:02,540 --> 00:44:06,000
The example is clear for the sales update meeting.

768
00:44:06,240 --> 00:44:11,750
Let's prepare some talking points on the new lead generation strategy focuses the prep.

769
00:44:11,770 --> 00:44:14,640
This is where strategic communication really gets tactical.

770
00:44:14,940 --> 00:44:16,060
Talking points aren't just.

771
00:44:16,260 --> 00:44:18,020
you know, a random list of things to say.

772
00:44:18,160 --> 00:44:18,340
No.

773
00:44:18,580 --> 00:44:19,360
What are they then?

774
00:44:19,380 --> 00:44:28,350
They are the concise, impactful, core messages you absolutely, positively want to land, regardless of how the conversation ebbs and flows.

775
00:44:28,470 --> 00:44:30,470
Okay, so they're like your anchors in the conversation.

776
00:44:30,590 --> 00:44:31,730
Exactly, your mental anchors.

777
00:44:32,300 --> 00:44:40,240
They ensure that even if the discussion goes off on a tangent, which meetings often do, you can skillfully, gently steer it back to your essential messages.

778
00:44:40,320 --> 00:44:41,770
Right, bring it back to what you need to say.

779
00:44:41,870 --> 00:44:43,870
And the process of preparing them.

780
00:44:43,850 --> 00:44:46,670
It forces you to distill complex information.

781
00:44:47,010 --> 00:44:50,620
You have to boil it down into clear, concise, digestible ideas.

782
00:44:51,080 --> 00:44:54,160
That skill alone is invaluable in fast-paced meetings.

783
00:44:54,300 --> 00:44:55,300
Where clarity is king.

784
00:44:55,320 --> 00:44:56,280
Absolutely.

785
00:44:56,860 --> 00:45:02,750
And for you, the learner, having those pre-planned points, it's a massive confidence booster.

786
00:45:02,850 --> 00:45:04,800
It gives you that mental roadmap.

787
00:45:04,820 --> 00:45:11,640
It makes it so much easier to speak articulately, confidently, persuasively, even if you're under pressure or the topic is tricky.

788
00:45:11,810 --> 00:45:18,140
So it turns potentially rambling thoughts into structured, impactful contributions.

789
00:45:18,160 --> 00:45:18,840
Perfectly put.

790
00:45:18,940 --> 00:45:23,210
It sounds like you're really delivering a planned message, not just reacting spontaneously.

791
00:45:23,230 --> 00:45:30,320
It feels like a secret weapon for speaking well, making sure your key insights are actually heard and understood, not just lost in the general chat.

792
00:45:30,300 --> 00:45:30,910
It really is.

793
00:45:31,190 --> 00:45:34,450
Preparing talking points allows you to control your message strategically.

794
00:45:34,900 --> 00:45:39,570
It ensures your valuable contributions land effectively rather than getting diluted or missed.

795
00:45:40,090 --> 00:45:43,300
It's about being intentional, impactful, memorable.

796
00:45:43,740 --> 00:45:44,500
Make your words count.

797
00:45:44,580 --> 00:45:45,560
Okay, that's powerful.

798
00:45:45,660 --> 00:45:51,170
So talking coins are strong, but what makes them even stronger in almost any business context?

799
00:45:51,190 --> 00:45:51,430
Data.

800
00:45:51,890 --> 00:45:52,470
Has to be data.

801
00:45:52,670 --> 00:45:53,180
Exactly.

802
00:45:53,220 --> 00:45:57,120
Which leads us right into section three, gathering your arsenal data and visuals.

803
00:45:57,180 --> 00:46:00,290
Because numbers, presented well, they don't just talk, they shout.

804
00:46:00,470 --> 00:46:01,630
They give weight to your arguments.

805
00:46:01,990 --> 00:46:02,610
Couldn't agree more.

806
00:46:03,170 --> 00:46:04,850
Data is the objective bedrock.

807
00:46:05,880 --> 00:46:08,800
Opinions are fine, but data provides the proof.

808
00:46:08,950 --> 00:46:11,190
And our sixth phrase captures this perfectly.

809
00:46:11,290 --> 00:46:14,380
It's concise, professional, I've pulled the latest numbers.

810
00:46:14,540 --> 00:46:16,140
Very common, very effective phrase.

811
00:46:16,380 --> 00:46:22,130
The guide explains pulled as a standard business term for gathered or collected data.

812
00:46:22,630 --> 00:46:27,510
Could be stats, sales figures, profit margins, market share, customer feedback scores.

813
00:46:28,660 --> 00:46:29,900
You name it.

814
00:46:29,920 --> 00:46:30,970
Quantitative evidence.

815
00:46:31,050 --> 00:46:36,380
And usually this data gets compiled into spreadsheets, graphs, maybe slides in a presentation.

816
00:46:36,440 --> 00:46:37,990
The example is spot on.

817
00:46:38,570 --> 00:46:40,700
I've pulled the latest numbers from last quarter's report.

818
00:46:41,140 --> 00:46:42,280
Instantly adds credibility.

819
00:46:42,630 --> 00:46:45,980
And what's really fascinating is the authority it conveys instantly.

820
00:46:46,220 --> 00:46:46,740
Right.

821
00:46:46,760 --> 00:46:52,130
In today's business world, decisions should be data driven, not based on gut feelings or anecdotes.

822
00:46:52,610 --> 00:46:57,660
So when you say confidently, I've pulled the latest numbers, you immediately signal that your input is fact based.

823
00:46:57,720 --> 00:46:58,280
It's credible.

824
00:46:58,340 --> 00:46:59,360
It's thoroughly researched.

825
00:46:59,380 --> 00:47:00,840
It shows you've done the work.

826
00:47:01,030 --> 00:47:01,590
Exactly.

827
00:47:01,950 --> 00:47:03,510
And the verb itself, pulled.

828
00:47:03,830 --> 00:47:04,330
It's succinct.

829
00:47:04,650 --> 00:47:05,350
It's professional.

830
00:47:05,670 --> 00:47:08,840
It implies a systematic, efficient process of getting that data.

831
00:47:09,200 --> 00:47:11,020
It avoids wishy-washy language.

832
00:47:11,080 --> 00:47:12,760
Better than saying, I looked at some numbers.

833
00:47:12,780 --> 00:47:13,500
Oh, much better.

834
00:47:14,220 --> 00:47:22,720
It positions you as someone who's done their homework, who has tangible evidence, who's ready to present and defend findings to clients, to your boss, to colleagues.

835
00:47:23,300 --> 00:47:25,060
It lends immediate gravitas.

836
00:47:25,170 --> 00:47:26,340
Yeah, I can see that.

837
00:47:26,380 --> 00:47:27,120
Think about the difference.

838
00:47:27,140 --> 00:47:35,410
Like you said, I think Q3 was pretty good versus I've pulled the latest numbers that Q3 revenue was up 15 percent, driven mainly by the X product line.

839
00:47:35,510 --> 00:47:36,310
Night and day, isn't it?

840
00:47:37,010 --> 00:47:42,240
The second one commands attention, shifts the conversation from vague feelings to objective analysis.

841
00:47:42,840 --> 00:47:46,520
You can debate why it's up 15 percent, but the fact itself is established.

842
00:47:46,500 --> 00:47:49,510
So it's about having verifiable facts, not just opinions.

843
00:47:49,770 --> 00:47:51,910
Demonstrating performance with evidence.

844
00:47:51,930 --> 00:47:53,070
Huge confidence booster.

845
00:47:53,510 --> 00:47:54,760
Massive credibility builder.

846
00:47:55,060 --> 00:47:55,540
Absolutely.

847
00:47:55,820 --> 00:48:06,670
So for you, the listener, mastering this phrase, being able to say it confidently because you have actually pulled the numbers, it demonstrates your analytical skills, your preparedness, your commitment to evidence.

848
00:48:07,160 --> 00:48:10,740
It makes your arguments far more persuasive, far more impactful.

849
00:48:11,020 --> 00:48:13,720
It's a direct route to being taken seriously.

850
00:48:13,700 --> 00:48:17,270
Okay, so you've got the crucial data, you've analyzed it, maybe compiled it.

851
00:48:17,650 --> 00:48:21,440
Now how do you best present it, especially if it's complex, in a meeting setting?

852
00:48:21,940 --> 00:48:22,200
Often.

853
00:48:22,520 --> 00:48:22,960
Visually.

854
00:48:24,060 --> 00:48:26,000
Slides are still king for complex data.

855
00:48:26,340 --> 00:48:27,050
Right.

856
00:48:27,070 --> 00:48:27,970
Presentation slides.

857
00:48:28,750 --> 00:48:30,890
Which takes us smoothly to phrase number seven.

858
00:48:31,050 --> 00:48:32,390
Let me run through the slides one more time.

859
00:48:32,930 --> 00:48:33,660
The final check.

860
00:48:34,300 --> 00:48:35,580
Essential for presenters.

861
00:48:35,730 --> 00:48:41,060
Our guide explains to run through something means a quick review, often checking flow, catching errors.

862
00:48:41,640 --> 00:48:48,510
Here, it's specifically about your presentation slides, the visuals, text, graphs, making sure they're perfect, no mistakes.

863
00:48:48,790 --> 00:48:49,010
Ready.

864
00:48:50,210 --> 00:48:56,740
The guide even compares it to double checking, but focus specifically on the presentation itself, the visual flow example.

865
00:48:56,960 --> 00:49:00,250
Okay, team, let me just run through the slides one more time before the client arrives.

866
00:49:00,410 --> 00:49:04,440
And this phrase really highlights how critical visual communication is now.

867
00:49:05,240 --> 00:49:08,790
Well-designed, error-free slides aren't just nice-to-haves anymore.

868
00:49:09,230 --> 00:49:12,290
They're often essential to reinforce your message effectively.

869
00:49:12,950 --> 00:49:15,510
Because errors can really kill your credibility, right?

870
00:49:15,530 --> 00:49:15,890
Instantly.

871
00:49:16,110 --> 00:49:17,880
A glaring spelling mistake.

872
00:49:17,900 --> 00:49:19,220
A wrong number on a chart.

873
00:49:19,300 --> 00:49:20,520
Inconsistent formatting.

874
00:49:21,040 --> 00:49:25,870
It distracts the audience, undermines your professionalism, no matter how brilliant your spoken points are.

875
00:49:26,070 --> 00:49:26,930
It makes you look careless.

876
00:49:27,310 --> 00:49:27,810
Exactly.

877
00:49:28,410 --> 00:49:30,250
And beyond just accuracy...

878
00:49:30,230 --> 00:49:34,460
Running through the slides helps you, the presenter, practice your delivery.

879
00:49:35,000 --> 00:49:36,080
You check the transitions.

880
00:49:36,440 --> 00:49:38,940
Does the story flow logically from slide to slide?

881
00:49:39,700 --> 00:49:43,070
It leads to a much more polished, professional, impactful delivery.

882
00:49:43,250 --> 00:49:45,290
So it's about the content and the performance.

883
00:49:45,330 --> 00:49:45,530
Yes.

884
00:49:46,230 --> 00:49:49,100
And this phrase implies that final critical check.

885
00:49:49,200 --> 00:49:54,410
Even if you've reviewed them 10 times already, this is the last look to ensure everything is truly perfect.

886
00:49:55,190 --> 00:49:56,850
It shows that commitment to excellence.

887
00:49:56,880 --> 00:50:02,970
That last-minute polish, catching that one typo or that awkwardly cropped image before it's projected for everyone to see.

888
00:50:03,390 --> 00:50:05,190
Exactly that feeling of relief.

889
00:50:05,850 --> 00:50:10,580
For you, the listener, using this phrase confidently shows your dedication to presenting well.

890
00:50:11,140 --> 00:50:18,330
It ensures your message is delivered flawlessly, visually reinforcing your professionalism, leaving that strong, positive impression.

891
00:50:18,770 --> 00:50:20,460
It signals high standards.

892
00:50:20,710 --> 00:50:21,350
Okay, great.

893
00:50:21,630 --> 00:50:22,950
So content is ready.

894
00:50:22,970 --> 00:50:24,290
Data is pulled.

895
00:50:24,790 --> 00:50:26,410
Visuals are polished.

896
00:50:26,430 --> 00:50:27,780
Now we need to think about the people.

897
00:50:28,120 --> 00:50:31,580
Specifically, who else needs to be in this meeting to make it really successful?

898
00:50:32,100 --> 00:50:33,270
Ah, the attendee list.

899
00:50:33,930 --> 00:50:35,830
Often overlooked, but critical.

900
00:50:36,310 --> 00:50:37,650
Which brings us to section four.

901
00:50:37,890 --> 00:50:40,450
Optimizing participation, who needs to be there?

902
00:50:40,970 --> 00:50:46,400
Because having the right mix of people in the room or on the call can be the difference between success and failure.

903
00:50:46,760 --> 00:50:47,200
Absolutely.

904
00:50:47,600 --> 00:50:51,910
Strategic orchestration of attendees, you could call it, vital for outcomes.

905
00:50:52,410 --> 00:50:54,170
And our eighth phrase is perfect for this.

906
00:50:54,690 --> 00:50:56,280
It sounds modern, collaborative.

907
00:50:56,820 --> 00:50:58,320
Do we need to loop anyone else in?

908
00:50:58,540 --> 00:51:00,480
Very current, very useful business phrase.

909
00:51:00,670 --> 00:51:02,550
Our research confirms this is widely used.

910
00:51:02,950 --> 00:51:11,750
To loop someone in means telling them about the meeting, including them, wanting them involved, and the opposite, if you're not included, you're out of the loop, which nobody likes to be.

911
00:51:11,890 --> 00:51:12,070
No.

912
00:51:12,840 --> 00:51:15,040
So this phrase is about proactive inclusion.

913
00:51:15,680 --> 00:51:16,580
Example.

914
00:51:16,560 --> 00:51:21,830
Before we finalize the plan, do we need to loop anyone else in from marketing or legal?

915
00:51:22,350 --> 00:51:27,340
And this phrase signals a really proactive, strategically smart approach to managing stakeholders.

916
00:51:27,960 --> 00:51:30,540
It's not just about adding names to an invite list.

917
00:51:31,040 --> 00:51:32,000
What's the strategy?

918
00:51:32,140 --> 00:51:36,390
It's about carefully identifying who else might be absolutely critical for success.

919
00:51:36,930 --> 00:51:38,750
Maybe you need their specific input.

920
00:51:38,790 --> 00:51:40,450
Maybe you need their decision-making power.

921
00:51:40,910 --> 00:51:44,400
Or maybe they simply need to be informed so they can take action afterwards.

922
00:51:44,480 --> 00:51:46,520
Okay, so it's purposeful inclusion.

923
00:51:46,630 --> 00:51:47,300
Exactly.

924
00:51:47,540 --> 00:51:49,100
And think about the problems it prevents.

925
00:51:49,400 --> 00:51:50,740
Information silos.

926
00:51:51,120 --> 00:51:54,310
When someone isn't looped in, they feel, well, out of the loop.

927
00:51:54,850 --> 00:51:59,910
And that almost always leads to delays, rework later on, maybe even resentment.

928
00:51:59,930 --> 00:52:01,150
Why wasn't I told about this?

929
00:52:01,170 --> 00:52:03,090
Right, that feeling of being blindsided.

930
00:52:03,150 --> 00:52:05,700
This simple question helps stop that before it happens.

931
00:52:05,960 --> 00:52:08,360
It makes sure all the necessary perspectives are at the table.

932
00:52:08,720 --> 00:52:11,100
It ensures maximum informed contribution.

933
00:52:11,640 --> 00:52:13,600
It encourages that collaborative mindset.

934
00:52:13,890 --> 00:52:20,690
So you're making sure everyone who needs to be there is there, avoiding those, if only we'd asked Sarah earlier.

935
00:52:20,670 --> 00:52:21,250
Moments.

936
00:52:21,890 --> 00:52:26,200
Or having to have a whole separate meeting just because someone key was missed first time around.

937
00:52:26,320 --> 00:52:26,920
Precisely.

938
00:52:27,560 --> 00:52:32,030
You're optimizing the meeting for success from the start by having the right people involved.

939
00:52:32,770 --> 00:52:40,840
Using this phrase shows your foresight, your ability to see the bigger picture, the organizational context, the interdependencies.

940
00:52:40,860 --> 00:52:42,110
It makes you look strategic.

941
00:52:42,090 --> 00:52:42,630
Definitely.

942
00:52:42,930 --> 00:52:49,380
For you, the listener, it positions you as a valuable player in effective team communication and collaboration.

943
00:52:49,840 --> 00:52:53,870
You ensure decisions are well-informed, inclusive, have the necessary buy-in.

944
00:52:54,430 --> 00:52:56,930
It shows you think beyond just your own tasks.

945
00:52:57,510 --> 00:52:58,520
You see the whole system.

946
00:52:58,840 --> 00:53:00,360
That's a really valuable phrase.

947
00:53:00,380 --> 00:53:04,670
Okay, now say you've got the right people, the meeting happens, or maybe you're just preparing some background.

948
00:53:05,740 --> 00:53:10,580
How do you efficiently summarize things for those involved or maybe for stakeholders who couldn't make it?

949
00:53:10,740 --> 00:53:11,780
